Table 1. The PBDB-T: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) control device showed a short-circuit current density \(\left(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\right)\) of \(12.80\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}\) , an open-circuit voltage \(\left(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\right)\) of \(0.853\ \mathrm{V},\) and a f ill factor (FF) of 0.682, yielding a PCE of \(7.45\%\) , which coincides with the result obtained in our previous work.[  36,39,40] The PBDB-T:ITIC device showed a \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(16.80\,\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}\) , an open-circuit voltage \(\left(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\right)\) of \(0.899\ \mathrm{V},\) and an FF of 0.742, yielding a PCE of \(11.21\%\) . The external quantum eff iciencies \((h_{\mathrm{EQE}})\) as a function of the wavelength of the incident light of the two devices are shown in Figure  2 b. Clearly, the PBDB-T:ITIC-based device exhibits a much broader response to sunlight, and the signif icantly improved \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) can be attributed to the light absorption of IITC. To further understand the \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) and FF of the PBDB-T:ITIC device, we measured the electron and hole mobilities using the space-charge-limited current method with the device structures of ITO/ZnO/PBDB-T:ITIC/Al and ITO/PEDOT:PSS/PBDB-T:ITIC/Au, respectively. The electron and hole mobilities were \(3.13\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\) and \(2.10\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\) , respectively (see Figure S3, Supporting Information). The relatively low ratio of electron and hole mobility \((\mu_{\mathrm{e}}/\mu_{\mathrm{h}}=1.49)\) suggests a fairly balanced charge transport in PBDB-T:ITIC blend f ilm, and thus a high \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) and FF can be obtained in PBDB-T:ITIC-based devices.

In order to understand whether there is further room to improve the photovoltage, which is another key parameter which determines the device performance, we directly probed the interfacial energetics through the charge-transfer (CT) state, which is an intermolecular state formed between the donor and acceptor.[  43,44]  Figure  3 shows the CT region of the EQE spectra and f its the CT band using Equation (1)

\[
\mathrm{EQE}(E)\propto\frac{1}{E\sqrt{4\pi\lambda kT}}\exp\left(\frac{-(E_{\mathrm{CT}}+\lambda-E)^2}{4\lambda kT}\right)
\]

where \(k\) is the Boltzmann’s constant, \(T\) is the absolute temperature, \(E_{\mathrm{CT}}\) is the energy of CT states, and \(\lambda\) is related to the width of the CT absorbance band, with contributions from internal/environmental reorganization and/or energetic disorder. Advanced spectroscopic studies have suggested that it is desirable to have material combinations with low reorganization energies so that delocalized charge wave functions can be obtained.[  45]  Recent investigations of intermolecular arrangements emphasized the importance of a specif ic molecular conf iguration at the donor/acceptor interfaces to decrease the energetic disorder and increase the charge separation.[  46]  Our f itting shows an obvious difference in the values of \(\lambda\) \((0.13~\mathrm{eV}\)

for the PBDB-T:ITIC blend and \(0.30~\mathrm{eV}\) for the PBDB-T: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) blend). The low \(\lambda\) value of \(0.13~\mathrm{eV}\) of our PBDB-T:ITIC blend represents one of the narrowest CT absorbance bands measured in any BHJ system and suggests eff icient charge generation regardless of its origin.[  46]

The \(E_{\mathrm{CT}}\) values from the f itting are 1.50 and \(1.42\ \mathrm{~eV}\) for the PBDB-T:ITIC and PBDB-T: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) devices, respectively. The difference in the CT energy \((1.50{-}1.42\,{=}\,0.08\,\mathrm{eV})\) is consistent with the difference in the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\left(0.06\,\mathrm{V}\right)\) of these two devices, and both are in good agreement with the empirical relation of \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\approx E_{\mathrm{CT}}/q\,{-}\,0.6\) .[  47]

The energy loss resulting from charge transfer is given by the difference between the energy of the excited pure components \((E_{\mathrm{P}\lambda})\) and the \(E_{\mathrm{CT}}\) . The PBDB-T:ITIC blend shows a low \(E_{\mathrm{P}\lambda}{-}E_{\mathrm{CT}}\) of \(0.09\ \mathrm{~eV}\) (1.59–1.50), which is among the lowest energy losses measured for any eff icient charge-transfer process.[  48–50] In contrast, for the PBDB-T: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) blend, the \(E_{\mathrm{P}\lambda}{-}E_{\mathrm{CT}}\) is as large as \(0.35~\mathrm{eV}\) (1.77–1.42 eV). The smaller \(E_{\mathrm{P}\lambda}{-}E_{\mathrm{CT}}\) in the PBDB-T:ITIC blend also explains its higher \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) value despite its smaller optical band gap relative to the PBDB-T: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) blend. With such a low \(E_{\mathrm{P}\lambda}{-}E_{\mathrm{CT}}\) further improvements in the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) can only be achieved by removing the nonradiative recombination pathways,[  44]  which would decrease the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) by

\[
\Delta V_{\mathrm{OC}}^{\mathrm{non-rad}}=-\frac{kT}{q}\ln(\mathrm{EQE}_{\mathrm{EL}})
\]

where \(\mathrm{EQE_{EL}}\) is radiative quantum eff iciency of the solar cell when charge carriers are injected into the device in the dark. At room temperature, \(\Delta V_{\mathrm{OC}}^{\mathrm{non-rad}}\) increases by \(60\;\mathrm{mV}\) when the \(\mathrm{EQE_{EL}}\) decreases by one order. The \(\mathrm{EQE_{EL}}\) of the pure ITIC device is measured as \(0.8\times10^{-3}\) , and that of the polymer:nonfullerene blend is \(1\times10^{-6}\) (see Figure S4, Supporting Information). The signif icant difference in the \(\mathrm{EQE_{EL}}\) values between the pure ITIC device and the PBDB-T:ITIC device constitutes the main contribution to the difference between the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) values of these

